From: "Claude (agent session)" <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:04:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: add the Direct3D 11 backend (ADR 0002)

Adds `directx11` to the Backend enum, defaulted on Windows, and implements
the GraphicsAPI contract from renderer/generic.zig in src/renderer/D3D11.zig
plus src/renderer/directx11/.

Windows had no usable GPU backend: Metal is Darwin-only and the OpenGL
backend is a compile-only stub for the embedded apprt that needs desktop
GL 4.3. The DLL now imports d3d11.dll and GenericRenderer(D3D11) compiles,
so the renderer runs end to end on Windows for the first time.

Verified against harness/hwnd-host: the surface clears to the terminal's
configured background (#282C34 by default, #FF0000 with --background=ff0000),
survives repeated resizes between 520x420 and 1400x900, and renders
identically on hardware and on forced WARP, both at feature level 11_1.

What is real
------------
Device creation walking ADR 0002's degradation ladder (hardware -> WARP),
the hwnd-mode swap chain, render targets, textures, samplers, dynamic
buffers, resize, device-lost detection on present, and clear + present.
drawFrame runs to completion and produces a correctly cleared, correctly
sized surface.

What is not
-----------
The cell pipelines, the glyph atlas draw path, and the HLSL shader set are
PLAN Phase 3. RenderPass.step accepts steps and ignores them - one of the two
`phase1_skeleton` markers in the tree. Everything else on the path is real,
which is why the generic renderer is wired up rather than bypassed: the
compiler checks the whole contract against actual call sites even though
nothing draws yet.

Composition mode - the DirectComposition surface handle a XAML
SwapChainPanel consumes, and the mode Windows Terminal will use - is Phase
2. init() returns UnsupportedSurface for it rather than silently presenting
nowhere.

Three things only running it could find
---------------------------------------
- DXGI rejects DXGI_ALPHA_MODE_PREMULTIPLIED for hwnd swap chains with
  DXGI_ERROR_INVALID_CALL. Transparency only means something when a
  compositor is behind the surface, so alpha mode now follows the
  presentation target: IGNORE for a window, PREMULTIPLIED for composition.
- generic.zig clears each pass to transparent black and paints the real
  background with the bg_color *pipeline*, explicitly avoiding clear_color so
  it need not do colour-space conversion on the CPU. With draw steps stubbed
  that leaves a black window, indistinguishable from a broken swap chain. The
  second `phase1_skeleton` marker clears to the terminal background instead,
  taken from the same uniforms the shader would have read - and has to apply
  the sRGB transfer function itself, because the target is an _SRGB format
  under linear blending and ClearRenderTargetView re-encodes on store.
  Without that conversion #282C34 rendered as #6E737D.
- GenericRenderer learns about size changes solely by comparing
  api.surfaceSize() against its own state. Returning the device's cached
  dimensions meant a grown window never reached the renderer and the newly
  exposed region stayed blank. drawFrameStart now re-syncs the swap chain
  from the window's client rect, which drawFrame calls immediately before
  consulting surfaceSize().

Notes on the harvest
--------------------
The COM/D3D11/DXGI bindings come from PR #11886 by deblasis (MIT), noted in
each file header. That PR wired only the vtable slots its SwapChainPanel
demo called and left the rest as `Reserved`, so this completes them:
CreateSwapChainForHwnd, CreateTexture2D, CreateShaderResourceView,
CreateSamplerState, CopyResource, UpdateSubresource, and the descriptor
types they take. Its device.zig was not reusable at all - it hardcoded
D3D_DRIVER_TYPE.HARDWARE with no fallback and took an ISwapChainPanelNative
- so device.zig here is new.

Details worth keeping in mind
-----------------------------
- The GPU data structs in directx11/shaders.zig are byte-identical to
  metal/shaders.zig, alignments included. GenericRenderer writes the same
  bytes for every backend, so drift there is silent corruption rather than
  a compile error. Metal's @sizeOf(CellText) == 32 test is carried over.
- present() copies the target to the back buffer instead of rendering
  straight into it, which is what keeps the Phase 6 post-process chain and
  Phase 7 damage gating possible without restructuring.
- RenderPass.Step.Draw.type is a backend-neutral Primitive enum mapped to
  D3D topology at draw time, so D3D vocabulary stays out of generic.zig.
- shadertoy.Target gains `hlsl`, whose conversion returns an explicit error.
  SPIRV-Cross has an HLSL backend, so Phase 6 is wiring rather than missing
  capability; refusing beats silently dropping a user's shader.
- GHOSTTY_D3D11_DRIVER=warp|hardware pins the rasterizer, which is how the
  forced-WARP exit criterion gets exercised on a machine with a working GPU.

zig build -Dapp-runtime=none -> ghostty-internal.dll, imports d3d11.dll,
                                234 exports
zig build test                -> 3065 passed, 55 skipped, 0 failed
                                (4 new: CellText size, DriverKind mapping,
                                 and the COM layer tests)

